  "worked_solution": "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $65%$ in account $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.15,\\,P(S|{A}^{\u2032})=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =(0.15)(0.35)+(0.25)(0.65) \\\\  & =0.215.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
